title "Undo for deleted links"
why   "People delete links by accident and had no way to get them back"
from  diff, tests, session

screen   page  "Links page"
endpoint api   "Links API"
table    links "links"
test     tests "API tests"
page -> api "lists, adds, deletes"
api -> links
tests -> api

~ table links "delete hides a link instead of erasing it"
    + deleted_at
    say "Deleting a link now hides it first, so it can still be brought back for a short time."
    eng "DELETE /api/links/:id still returns 204, but remove() now sets deleted_at (ms) instead of deleting the row; list() skips rows with deleted_at set. Existing databases get the column added on startup."
    proof db.js:34
    proof db.js:19
    proof test deletes a link
    proof test upgrades an existing database without a deleted_at column
    said "People delete links by accident. Make delete undoable from the page for a few seconds."

+ endpoint restore "POST /api/links/:id/restore"
    say "There is a new way to ask the server to bring a just-deleted link back, with its original date."
    eng "db.restore(id) clears deleted_at and returns the link (same id and created_at), 200. Returns 404 if the link is live, unknown, or past the window."
    proof server.js:22
    proof db.js:37
    proof test restores a deleted link within the undo window
    proof test restoring a link that was never deleted or does not exist is a 404
+ page -> restore "Undo"
    proof public/index.html:45
+ restore -> links "clears deleted_at"
    proof db.js:39

~ links "kept for 30 seconds, then erased the next time anyone uses the app"
    + undo window 30 s
    say "The server keeps a deleted link for 30 seconds, much longer than the Undo button shows, so a slow phone still has time to undo. After that it is erased for good."
    eng "UNDO_MS = 30_000. Purge is lazy: list(), remove() and restore() first DELETE rows with deleted_at <= now - UNDO_MS. No timers on the server."
    proof db.js:5
    proof db.js:21
    proof test keeps a deleted link restorable for 30 seconds
    proof test refuses to restore after the undo window and purges the link
    said "But keep deleted links restorable for 30 seconds on the server, not 10, some of our users are on slow phones."
    said "Good that you purge lazily, no timers on the server."

+ part toast "Undo toast on the links page"
    say "After deleting, a small bar at the bottom of the page names the link and offers Undo for 5 seconds. Undo puts the link back and confirms it; if it is too late, the bar says so."
    eng "#toast with role=status, aria-live=polite; UNDO_SHOW_MS = 5000; the title is set with textContent; showToast() replaces any earlier toast."
    proof public/index.html:11
    proof public/index.html:26
    proof public/index.html:45
    said "Make delete undoable from the page for a few seconds."

= page "list rendering left as it was"
    say "The way the list of links is drawn was left alone on purpose."
    eng "load() still builds the list with innerHTML from url and title without escaping (an existing injection risk), to be fixed in a separate PR."
    proof public/index.html:15
    said "Leave the page's list rendering alone in this PR."

! toast "page behaviour has no automated test"
    say "The Undo bar was only tried by hand in a browser, once, before the server window went from 10 to 30 seconds. It worked then: the link disappeared, the bar showed, Undo brought it back, and the bar went away after 5 seconds. Also, only the latest delete can be undone; deleting a second link replaces the bar. Nobody asked for that limit."
    eng "Browser check drove the real buttons on localhost; the 30 s change is a server constant the page does not read, so it was not re-run. No test covers index.html."
    proof public/index.html:35

! links "a deleted link lingers until the next request"
    say "A deleted link is not erased exactly at 30 seconds: it stays hidden in the database until the next time someone uses the app. Check that this is fine for anything that must really be gone."
    eng "Lazy purge means soft-deleted rows can sit in linkbox.db indefinitely on an idle server. That is reasoned from the code, not tested over time."
    proof db.js:21
